WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:02.901
-You know my next guest from
her Emmy-nominated work

00:00:02.901 --> 00:00:04.437
on "Stranger Things."

00:00:04.437 --> 00:00:07.106
Now, you can see her starring in
the new movie "Enola Holmes,"

00:00:07.106 --> 00:00:09.341
which is available
on Netflix now.

00:00:09.341 --> 00:00:11.510
Here's Millie Bobby Brown.

00:00:11.510 --> 00:00:15.414
Millie, thank you so much
for coming back on the show.

00:00:15.414 --> 00:00:19.084
And thank you for doing the
first Lip-sync Battle over Zoom.

00:00:19.084 --> 00:00:22.121
And congratulations on the win
and everything.

00:00:22.121 --> 00:00:25.391
-[ Laughs ] Thank you so much.
Thank you.

00:00:25.391 --> 00:00:28.094
I really -- I really worked
hard on that.

00:00:28.094 --> 00:00:30.230
Do you understand?
Three days I was sitting there

00:00:30.230 --> 00:00:33.165
thinking, "What should I do
for these songs?"

00:00:33.165 --> 00:00:35.534
And -- and this is what
I came up with.

00:00:35.534 --> 00:00:38.704
-You crushed it. Hey.
I saw this tweet last night.

00:00:38.704 --> 00:00:41.207
Does this mean what I think
it means?

00:00:41.207 --> 00:00:45.144
Is season four of "Stranger
Things" in production?

00:00:45.144 --> 00:00:47.180
-Um, I mean, I guess that's --

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:49.181
guess that's what they're
trying to say.

00:00:49.181 --> 00:00:50.883
[ Laughs ]

00:00:50.883 --> 00:00:52.751
Very obviously, I just
can't wait.

00:00:52.751 --> 00:00:54.487
I can't wait to get back
to work.

00:00:54.487 --> 00:00:56.756
I miss playing Eleven so much.

00:00:56.756 --> 00:00:59.292
And, you know, to think
it's been quite a while

00:00:59.292 --> 00:01:01.794
since I've just been on set
with my friends again.

00:01:01.794 --> 00:01:04.330
-Yeah.
-And just playing Eleven.

00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:07.133
You know, she's always such
a challenge to play.

00:01:07.133 --> 00:01:09.902
And I just miss work,
to be honest with you.

00:01:09.902 --> 00:01:12.071
-I heard that -- I don't know
if this is true but

00:01:12.071 --> 00:01:14.440
before you got the role in
"Stranger Things" as Eleven,

00:01:14.440 --> 00:01:17.477
you were about to quit acting?

00:01:17.477 --> 00:01:21.113
-I was -- I think I was just
very disheartened

00:01:21.113 --> 00:01:24.083
by the rejection, which is
something I tell everyone is,

00:01:24.083 --> 00:01:28.421
like, this industry is such --
is just full of rejection, 24/7.

00:01:28.421 --> 00:01:30.022
-It really is.
-Yeah.

00:01:30.022 --> 00:01:32.992
I feel like you do get far
more -- I mean, you get nos,

00:01:32.992 --> 00:01:35.227
a lot of nos,
before you get a yes.

00:01:35.227 --> 00:01:38.163
And I was auditioning for
commercials,

00:01:38.163 --> 00:01:40.266
for anything, really.

00:01:40.266 --> 00:01:41.867
I, then, auditioned
for "Game of Thrones"

00:01:41.867 --> 00:01:43.703
and I got a "no" for that.

00:01:43.703 --> 00:01:45.571
And then, that's kind of
when I was like,

00:01:45.571 --> 00:01:47.140
"Oh, this is really
difficult."

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:50.009
And I guess I really wanted
that role.

00:01:50.009 --> 00:01:53.279
So one of my last kind of
goes at this all

00:01:53.279 --> 00:01:56.682
was -- was this Netflix show
called "Montauk."

00:01:56.682 --> 00:01:58.784
And I auditioned.

00:01:58.784 --> 00:02:00.819
And then, like, two months
later, they just --

00:02:00.819 --> 00:02:02.721
they got back to us, and was
like, "Hey," like,

00:02:02.721 --> 00:02:05.391
"We'd love to -- we'd love
to Skype with you."

00:02:05.391 --> 00:02:07.793
And then, I Skyped with them
and, you know,

00:02:07.793 --> 00:02:09.963
the rest is history, of course.

00:02:09.963 --> 00:02:11.397
We then named the show
"Stranger Things"

00:02:11.397 --> 00:02:14.267
and that's -- blah, blah, blah.
But, you know, "Montauk"

00:02:14.267 --> 00:02:17.169
was definitely the one that
kind of that hope

00:02:17.169 --> 00:02:19.672
of doing it all again.

00:02:19.672 --> 00:02:20.907
-And now, you're -- look at you.

00:02:20.907 --> 00:02:22.675
You are starring in a new film
for Netflix

00:02:22.675 --> 00:02:26.613
called "Enola Holmes,"
which I loved, by the way.

00:02:26.613 --> 00:02:28.247
-Yay!
-It is fantastic.

00:02:28.247 --> 00:02:31.184
My girls are going to flip.
Love it.

00:02:31.184 --> 00:02:33.686
I'm going to show it to them
this weekend.

00:02:33.686 --> 00:02:35.554
It's getting crazy
great reviews.

00:02:35.554 --> 00:02:37.957
92% fresh on Rotten Tomatoes.

00:02:37.957 --> 00:02:40.293
It's reached number one
in most countries.

00:02:40.293 --> 00:02:42.728
Has -- it -- it's so charming.
I loved it.

00:02:42.728 --> 00:02:44.897
I can't even tell you.
Henry Cavill --

00:02:44.897 --> 00:02:46.965
-I love this.
This is so awesome.

00:02:46.965 --> 00:02:50.769
-I want to go on and on.
Henry Cavill's fantastic in it.

00:02:50.769 --> 00:02:52.772
Helena Bonham Carter.

00:02:52.772 --> 00:02:54.907
I mean, how great, her acting?

00:02:54.907 --> 00:02:58.110
She does like 25 faces in one
face for every like --

00:02:58.110 --> 00:02:59.745
-Amazing. I -- just to even --

00:02:59.745 --> 00:03:02.815
just to say I've worked with her
is just an absolute honor.

00:03:02.815 --> 00:03:04.483
And she is, you know, I think,

00:03:04.483 --> 00:03:08.154
one of the best female actors
in the whole entire world.

00:03:08.154 --> 00:03:11.490
And just to have her play
my mother was just an honor.

00:03:11.490 --> 00:03:14.126
And then, we have Sam Claflin
and Henry Cavill,

00:03:14.126 --> 00:03:15.461
which was just amazing.

00:03:15.461 --> 00:03:18.498
I think we had such an amazing
lineup of cast.

00:03:18.498 --> 00:03:21.100
-Everyone knocked it
out of the park on this one,

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:22.468
I got to say.
It was great.

00:03:22.468 --> 00:03:25.170
And you -- you've -- you were
a producer on this?

00:03:25.170 --> 00:03:26.572
How -- how was that experience?

00:03:26.572 --> 00:03:27.673
How'd you get --
-Yes.

00:03:27.673 --> 00:03:29.308
-...to be a producer?
-Yeah.

00:03:29.308 --> 00:03:32.511
Well, the -- the idea really
came to my family and I

00:03:32.511 --> 00:03:35.648
when my older sister, Paige,
read the book series.

00:03:35.648 --> 00:03:40.520
And after, kind of, feeling so
immersed by the story of Enola,

00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:44.056
we wanted to bring this to,
you know, screens

00:03:44.056 --> 00:03:46.025
for people to see, visually.
-Yeah.

00:03:46.025 --> 00:03:51.130
So, my dad, with our production
company, PCMA Productions,

00:03:51.130 --> 00:03:53.466
we found a studio.

00:03:53.466 --> 00:03:55.100
We found a writer.
We found a director.

00:03:55.100 --> 00:03:58.037
And we just pushed for this film
to be made.

00:03:58.037 --> 00:04:02.674
I think, for me, I was
on this journey,

00:04:02.674 --> 00:04:04.677
you know,
in the production side.

00:04:04.677 --> 00:04:08.147
So it only felt right for me to
produce, and to be able to

00:04:08.147 --> 00:04:11.817
kind of be, not only on screen
but off of camera.

00:04:11.817 --> 00:04:14.921
And just to have an opportunity
like that was amazing

00:04:14.921 --> 00:04:18.457
for, you know, young filmmakers,
like, you know, me who just --

00:04:18.457 --> 00:04:20.793
I do want to make films,

00:04:20.793 --> 00:04:24.196
and I've always been
really into that,

00:04:24.196 --> 00:04:26.098
as well as being on screen.
-Yeah.

00:04:26.098 --> 00:04:28.300
-So, having that opportunity
was awesome.

00:04:28.300 --> 00:04:29.769
-You get to learn so much.

00:04:29.769 --> 00:04:33.906
I mean, but also, I love hearing
you having a --

00:04:33.906 --> 00:04:37.476
an English accent in a movie
because you're so --

00:04:37.476 --> 00:04:39.912
you're so good at doing
American, that I'm like,

00:04:39.912 --> 00:04:41.247
"Oh, she gets to do that."
-Yeah.

00:04:41.247 --> 00:04:44.217
-Was it easier?
-Um, no,

00:04:44.217 --> 00:04:45.818
because I like to just go
straight into

00:04:45.818 --> 00:04:47.553
my American accent,
and I can just be like,

00:04:47.553 --> 00:04:49.788
[ American accent ] "Hi," like,
"My name's Millie Bobby Brown."

00:04:49.788 --> 00:04:51.624
[ English accent ] And then,
when I have to focus on

00:04:51.624 --> 00:04:54.660
doing an English accent,
I was like, "Huh,"

00:04:54.660 --> 00:04:58.530
because I had to say...

00:04:58.530 --> 00:04:59.965
"advertisements," right?

00:04:59.965 --> 00:05:01.501
So, I usually say
advertisements,

00:05:01.501 --> 00:05:03.202
but in England, you say
advert-isements.

00:05:03.202 --> 00:05:04.670
-Advert-isements, yeah.

00:05:04.670 --> 00:05:06.705
-For instance, you know,
here you say privacy.

00:05:06.705 --> 00:05:08.474
In England, you say priv-acy.

00:05:08.474 --> 00:05:11.010
So, those types of things.
I grew up in America.

00:05:11.010 --> 00:05:13.946
So this is like -- I'm very much
used to that.

00:05:13.946 --> 00:05:16.515
And doing an American accent's
a lot easier

00:05:16.515 --> 00:05:18.117
than doing an English, I think.

00:05:18.117 --> 00:05:20.520
-How do you say controversy?

00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:22.355
-Controv-ersy.
You say controv-ersy.

00:05:22.355 --> 00:05:24.824
I say controversy.
But my -- my sister,

00:05:24.824 --> 00:05:26.025
in every meeting, she's like,

00:05:26.025 --> 00:05:27.460
"Well, that's, you know,
controv-ersy."

00:05:27.460 --> 00:05:29.494
And I'm like, "Hey, no."
[ Laughter ]

00:05:29.494 --> 00:05:31.564
-I love that.
You crushed it.

00:05:31.564 --> 00:05:34.266
And honestly, the fight scenes
were great.

00:05:34.266 --> 00:05:36.502
I -- I just --

00:05:36.502 --> 00:05:38.904
There wasn't one second I would
be like, "Eh, that was --"

00:05:38.904 --> 00:05:40.739
Everything was phenomenal.
I loved it.

00:05:40.739 --> 00:05:42.508
-Yeah. Thank you.
-Congrats.

00:05:42.508 --> 00:05:44.544
I think you have a franchise
on your hands, buddy.

00:05:44.544 --> 00:05:47.212
It was just awesome.
-Ask Netflix, please.

00:05:47.212 --> 00:05:49.615
-Oh, don't even have to ask.
They're going to be begging.

00:05:49.615 --> 00:05:51.384
Are you kidding me?
"Enola Holmes."

00:05:51.384 --> 00:05:53.585
It's so good.
I want to show a clip.

00:05:53.585 --> 00:05:56.122
Here's Millie Bobby Brown
in "Enola Holmes."

00:05:56.122 --> 00:05:57.990
Take a look.

00:05:57.990 --> 00:06:00.493
[ Grunting ]

00:06:00.493 --> 00:06:03.929
♪♪

00:06:03.929 --> 00:06:06.498
[ Grunting continues ]

00:06:06.498 --> 00:06:08.167
-[ Gasps ]

00:06:08.167 --> 00:06:09.435
[ Grunts ]

00:06:09.435 --> 00:06:11.771
[ Panting ]

00:06:11.771 --> 00:06:21.747
♪♪

00:06:21.747 --> 00:06:28.287
♪♪

00:06:28.287 --> 00:06:30.589
-My thanks to
Millie Bobby Brown.

00:06:30.589 --> 00:06:33.793
"Enola Holmes" is available
now on Netflix.

00:06:33.793 --> 00:06:36.161
When we come back, BTS
is closing out

00:06:36.161 --> 00:06:37.964
an incredible week
of performances.

00:06:37.964 --> 00:06:40.199
Stick around. Thank you, Millie.

00:06:40.199 --> 00:06:42.068
-Thank you! Bye!

